    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `BashComplete._check_version`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_check_version` function is a static method within the `BashComplete` class. Its purpose is to verify that the version of the Bash shell being used is at least 4.4, as shell completion features require this minimum version. The function does not take any parameters. It uses the `subprocess` module to execute a Bash command that outputs the version of the Bash shell. The output is then decoded and matched against a regular expression to extract the major and minor version numbers. If the version is older than 4.4, or if the version cannot be detected, the function prints an error message to the standard error stream using the `echo` function from the `click` library. The function does not return any value; it only performs checks and outputs messages. It relies on the `re` module for regular expression matching and the `click` library for outputting messages. The function handles errors by checking if the regular expression match is `None` and by printing an appropriate error message in that case. There are no exceptions raised or early returns in the function. The side effect of this function is the potential output of error messages to the standard error stream if the Bash version is too old or cannot be detected.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed o